Object.freeze is shallow. It freezes only the immediate object—assigning to top-level properties fails. Nested objects are unfrozen, so mutations like `cfg.db.host = 'y'` succeed.

To lock the whole structure:
```javascript
const deepFreeze = (obj) => {
  Object.freeze(obj);
  Object.getOwnPropertyNames(obj).forEach(prop => {
    if (obj[prop] !== null && (typeof obj[prop] === "object" || typeof obj[prop] === "function")) {
      deepFreeze(obj[prop]);
    }
  });
  return obj;
};

const cfg = deepFreeze({ db: { host: 'x' } });
cfg.db.host = 'y'; // now fails silently (or throws in strict mode)
```

The assignment to `cfg.db` would still fail. But `cfg.db.host` only fails if you recursively freeze all reachable objects.
